From f1e24b45602ea20b0b8e1ef049e1d329693fc271 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 1 Aug 2026 20:26:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Let the store cache describe a property creation

The store inline cache could only ever describe overwriting a slot that
already exists. It records where a write landed - (shape after the write,
slot) - and hits through TryWriteShapeSlot, which requires the property to be
there. A store that CREATES its property therefore installs an entry keyed on
the shape the object ended up with, and the next object arrives with that
shape's predecessor. It misses. Always, at every such site, for the life of
the process: measured 0 store-cache hits against 600 000 misses for 200 000
constructions of a three-field object. Every constructor assigning fields one
at a time missed on every field of every object it ever built, which is the
exact shape of Richards' TaskControlBlock, DeltaBlue's constraints,
RayTrace's Vector and Box2D's b2Vec2.

Add a second entry form keyed on the shape BEFORE the write, discriminated by
a null FromShape exactly as the read cache discriminates own from prototype
entries. A hit creates the property in the recorded slot and advances the
object to the recorded successor shape, which is where the saving is:
TrackShapeDataProperty would look the key up in the current shape, miss, and
then look it up again in that shape's transition table to arrive at the very
shape and slot the entry already holds. Now 599 997 hits against 3 misses -
one cold miss per field - and ~20% faster on a 2 M-iteration constructor
loop, median of paired ratios 0.797 over four interleaved pairs, every pair
the same direction.

Creating a property is the last step of OrdinarySetWithOwnDescriptor, so a
replay is only correct under conditions the shape id says nothing about, and
three guards carry them.

A concrete shape proves the key is absent: while an object is in shape mode
its tracked keys ARE its complete set of own named properties, because every
untrackable addition - a private name, an accessor, non-default attributes, a
deferred cell - abandons the shape first. The entry holds the ObjectShape by
reference rather than by id, so the test is identity and Empty being shared by
every fresh object is harmless.

The prototype chain is walked once, at install, and required to supply
nothing for the key. It would change the outcome if it did: a setter there has
to run, and an inherited non-writable data property has to reject the write.
Two guards keep that answer true at every later hit - the receiver still
pointing at the same prototype, by reference, and the global
prototype-mutation version, which any addition to any object used as a
prototype publishes. Those are the same two the read cache's prototype form
uses, and they only hold up because the previous change stopped `new` from
advancing that version once per allocation; before it, a transition entry
retired on the next object the loop built.

Extensibility is re-checked on every hit, unlike the overwrite form which
deliberately omits it, because this call adds a property rather than
overwriting one. preventExtensions, seal and freeze all set NonExtensible, so
one test covers all three.

Everything else falls through to the unchanged generic path, and that is what
bounds the risk: a guard failing costs a miss, never a wrong answer, and a
rejected creation is reported by the generic path exactly as before. The only
way to be wrong is a false positive, and each guard closes one.

A creation is detected by the shape changing across the store. A shape is
immutable, so a receiver reporting a different one has gained a tracked
property, and the only one it can have gained at this site is this key - no
pre-lookup of the key is needed, which matters because it would be paid on
every miss.

PropertyStoreCacheTests covers each way either condition can stop holding,
each after the site has been warmed on the fast path: a prototype setter
present from the start and added mid-loop, an inherited read-only data
property in sloppy and strict mode, a non-extensible receiver in sloppy and
strict mode, a frozen receiver, two receivers sharing a shape but not a
prototype, setPrototypeOf mid-loop, `__proto__` still reaching the inherited
accessor, a dictionary-mode receiver, a Proxy as receiver and in the chain,
delete and re-create, attributes and key order, and the hit rate itself.
Removing the two hit-time prototype guards fails four of them.

test262 is NOT run here. The properties-proxy and strict-mode manifests are
owed for this item before it can close, per the measurement protocol and the
phase-2 exit gate.
